  "text_input": "A 73-year-old male presented with a one-day history of easy fatigability, giddiness, and vomiting. He is a known case of systemic hypertension (5 years) and bronchial asthma (15 years), with a history of smoking. Clinical examination revealed a blood pressure of 150/94 mmHg and a pulse of 92 bpm. Laboratory investigations were significant for an elevated ESR of 110 mm/hr and a peripheral smear showing normocytic normochromic anemia. Imaging and echocardiography revealed a subacute infarct in the right thalamus and mild left ventricular diastolic dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 55%. The patient was diagnosed with a cerebrovascular accident, hyponatremia secondary to SIADH, systemic hypertension, bronchial asthma, and a urinary tract infection caused by MDR Klebsiella. He was treated with antibiotics (Ciprofloxacin, Piperacillin/Tazobactam), antiplatelets, statins, and nebulization.",
